Creating a mask
The parameters inside the cv2.circle() function are the image, center of the circle, the radius of the circle, the color of the circle(255 represents black), and the thickness of the circle(-1 suggests that the circle is filled).

The parameters inside the cv2.rectangle function are the image, corner point of the bottom left corner, cornet point of the top right corner, the color of the rectangle(255 represents black), and the thickness of the rectangle(-1 suggests that the circle is filled).

Apply the mask to the image
To apply a mask to the image, we use the cv2.bitwise_and() function of the OpenCV library. This function requires three parameters. The first two parameters are the images where we’ll apply the AND bitwise operation. Since we already found the intersection of our images, we only need to input the same image twice. The third parameter is the mask, where we use the crescent-shaped mask we created.

masked = cv2.bitwise_and(img,img,mask=crescent_shape)
cv2.imshow('CrescentMask', masked)


